  "client_view_text": " In this statement, a \"crash\" is a situation in which the database is shut down abruptly (e.g. due to a power outage, a server failure, or a bug in the software).\nA write-ahead log (WAL) is a record of all modifications made to a database. When a modification is made to a database, the change is first written to the log, and then it is applied to the database. This ensures that the log is always up-to-date, even if the database itself is not. If the database crashes, the WAL can be used to recover the database to its previous state.\nWhen the database crashes, the WAL contains a record of all the modifications that were made, but not yet written to the database. These modifications can be reapplied to the database, restoring it to its previous state. This process is called recovery. During recovery, the database reads the WAL and reapplies the modifications, ensuring that the database is consistent and up-to-date.\nThe use of a WAL also provides an additional benefit: it allows the database to recover to a specific point in time, rather than just to a previous checkpoint. This means that the database can recover from a crash that occurred at any point in time, not just at a predetermined checkpoint."
